---
title: "SSRM Datasource"
enterprise: true
---

This section describes the Server-Side Datasource and demonstrates how it is used to load data from a server.

The Server-Side Row Model requires a datasource to fetch rows for the grid. When users scroll or perform grid operations
such as sorting or grouping, more data will be requested via the datasource.

{% note %}
Most of the Server-Side Row Model examples include a fake server that generates SQL to imitate how a real server
might use the requests sent from the grid. These examples use [AlaSQL](http://alasql.org/) which is a
JavaScript SQL database that works in browsers.

However, note that the Server-Side Row Model does not impose any restrictions on the server-side technologies used.
{% /note %}

## Enabling Server-Side Row Model

The [Client-Side Row Model](./row-models/#client-side) is the default Row Model. To use the SSRM instead, set the `rowModelType` as follows:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    rowModelType: 'serverSide',
}
```

## Implementing the Server-Side Datasource

A datasource is used by the SSRM to fetch rows for the grid.

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="IServerSideDatasource" /%}

The following snippet shows a simple datasource implementation:

```js
const createDatasource = server => {
    return {
        // called by the grid when more rows are required
        getRows: params => {

            // get data for request from server
            const response = server.getData(params.request);

            if (response.success) {
                // supply rows for requested block to grid
                params.success({
                    rowData: response.rows
                });
            } else {
                // inform grid request failed
                params.fail();
            }
        }
    };
}
```

Notice that the datasource contains a single method `getRows(params)` which is called by the grid when more rows are
required. A request is supplied in the `params` object which contains all the information required by the server to
fetch data from the server.

Rows fetched from the server are supplied to the grid via `params.success({ rowData: rows })`.

## Registering the Datasource

The datasource is registered with the grid via either a) the grid property `serverSideDatasource` or b) the grid API.

Registering the datasource via grid options is done as follows:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    serverSideDatasource: myDatasource
}
```

Alternatively, the datasource can be registered via the grid API:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
api.setGridOption('serverSideDatasource', myDatasource);
```

The example below demonstrates loading rows using a simple SSRM Datasource. Note the following:

- The Server-Side Row Model is selected using the grid options property: `rowModelType = 'serverSide'`.
- The datasource is registered with the grid using: `api.setGridOption('serverSideDatasource', datasource)`.
- The `getRows(params)` defines the request parameters, with `params` containing a `startRow` and `endRow` that determines the range of rows to return. For example, if the `getRows` function is called with `startRow: 0` and `endRow: 100`, then the grid will expect a result with 100 rows (rows 0 to 99).
- When scrolling down there is a delay as more rows are fetched from the server.
- See the console below the example to view the request data sent by the grid for rows.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Simple Server-Side Datasource" name="simple" /%}

## Reading Every Block From One Data Version

Each block of rows is fetched with a separate `getRows` call, so when the data changes on the server between calls, blocks of the same level can be read from different states of the data and rows can be repeated or skipped where the blocks meet. If the server can read from a fixed version of the data, such as a snapshot ID or a cursor, return it as `dataVersion` with the block. The grid pins the first version returned for a level and passes it back as `request.dataVersion` on every later `getRows` request for that level.

Pinning a version is snapshot behaviour: it suits paging through a dataset that does not change underneath the reader, so every block of a level shows the data as it was when the level was first read. This is the default `'snapshot'` mode of the `serverSideDataVersionMode` grid option. When the grid should show data that changes continuously, set `serverSideDataVersionMode` to `'stream'`, return the version of the rows with each block and send each change as a transaction with a `version`. See [Versioned Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#versioned-transactions).

```js
const datasource = {
    getRows: params => {
        // request.dataVersion is undefined until the level has a pinned version
        const response = server.getData(params.request);
        params.success({
            rowData: response.rows,
            rowCount: response.lastRow,
            dataVersion: response.snapshotId,
        });
    }
};
```

Note the following:

- Each level pins its own version: a group's children are read independently of their parent level.
- The pinned version is dropped when the level is [refreshed](./server-side-model-updating-refresh/) with `refreshServerSide`, purged or not, and when the level is reset, for example after a sort or filter change. The next response pins a new version. A response to a request sent before the refresh is discarded when the request or the response has a version, and its block is loaded again.
- The version is kept when blocks are evicted from the cache and loaded again, when failed loads are retried, when transactions are applied and when a group is collapsed. In `'stream'` mode, a newer version returned with a block or a newer [versioned transaction](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#versioned-transactions) moves the pinned version forward.
- Blocks requested before the first versioned response arrives (up to `maxConcurrentDatasourceRequests`) are sent without a version. If one of them returns a different version from the one pinned in the meantime, its rows are discarded and the block is loaded again with the pinned version.
- When a block requested with the pinned version returns a different version, the server can no longer read the pinned version, so the grid reloads the level as a purge refresh does and pins the version returned next. If a block returns a different version again before the level is refreshed or reset, the grid keeps the block's rows, keeps the version it pinned, and logs a warning.
- In `'stream'` mode, a block returned with a version older than the pinned version is loaded again instead, as described in [Versioned Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#versioned-transactions).
- Two numeric versions are compared numerically, and any other versions are only checked for equality. To decide when two versions are the same, for example versions that differ only in case, provide the `compareServerSideDataVersions` grid option. It returns a negative number when the first version is older than the second, a positive number when it is newer, and `0` when they are the same version.
- A version supplied through `applyServerSideRowData` pins the level in the same way.
- The pinned version of each level is returned by `getServerSideGroupLevelState()`.
